---
import SiteLayout from "../../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import FlavorTabs from "../../../components/FlavorTabs.astro";
import ModuleHero from "../../../components/module/ModuleHero.astro";
import ModuleScenario from "../../../components/module/ModuleScenario.astro";
import ModuleFlowDiagram from "../../../components/module/ModuleFlowDiagram.astro";
import AgentLoopDemo from "../../../components/module/demos/AgentLoopDemo.astro";
import ModuleFooterNav from "../../../components/module/ModuleFooterNav.astro";
import ModuleRecap from "../../../components/module/ModuleRecap.astro";
import ModuleQuiz from "../../../components/module/ModuleQuiz.astro";
import { flavor as gcp } from "../../../data/flavors/gcp";
import { flavor as aws } from "../../../data/flavors/aws";
import { flavor as azure } from "../../../data/flavors/azure";
import { flavor as oss } from "../../../data/flavors/oss";
import { MODULES } from "../../../data/modules";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const moduleId = "85-agents";
const entries = {
  gcp: gcp[moduleId],
  aws: aws[moduleId],
  azure: azure[moduleId],
  oss: oss[moduleId],
};
const moduleData = MODULES.find((m) => m.id === moduleId)!;
---

<SiteLayout title="85 · Agents & tool use" description={moduleData.summary}>
  <article>
    <ModuleHero moduleId={moduleId}>
      <p>
        Every module so far has been a single retrieval pass answering a single question. Agents are for when the
        task takes more than one step — and after evaluating, observing, securing, and costing out the pipeline, this
        is where you decide when that extra complexity is actually worth it.
      </p>
      <p>
        See the <a href={`${base}#agent-loop`}>plan/act/observe loop and single- vs. multi-agent patterns</a> on the
        homepage first if you want the concept in one diagram before this module's implementation detail.
      </p>
    </ModuleHero>

    <ModuleScenario moduleId={moduleId} />

    <ModuleFlowDiagram moduleId={moduleId} />

    <h2>Function calling</h2>
    <p>
      The mechanism underneath every agent: the model emits a structured request to call a tool (name + arguments),
      your runtime executes it — often the Module 25 retrieval call — and the result returns as new input. The model
      never runs code directly, only asks.
    </p>

    <h2>Agent loops: plan, act, observe</h2>
    <p>
      An <strong>agent loop</strong> repeats plan → act → observe until done. Left unbounded, a loop stuck on an
      unsolvable task keeps spinning — every agent needs a hard iteration or token budget that forces it to stop and
      report failure instead.
    </p>

    <AgentLoopDemo />

    <h2>MCP as a standard interface</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>MCP (Model Context Protocol)</strong> standardizes tool wiring: a tool implements one MCP server, any
      MCP-aware agent can call it — the same value a database driver provides, applied to agent tooling.
    </p>

    <h2>Agent development kits: buy the loop, don't build it</h2>
    <p class="concept-comparison-lede">Almost nobody hand-rolls the loop above. Four picks, four trade-offs:</p>
    <div class="concept-comparison" role="list">
      <div class="concept-comparison-item" role="listitem">
        <span class="chip chip--accent">LANGGRAPH</span>
        <p>Explicit state graph, deterministic edges, built-in human-approval interrupt, full audit trail.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="concept-comparison-item" role="listitem">
        <span class="chip">CREWAI</span>
        <p>Role-based agents, fastest multi-agent prototype — less control once workflows outgrow simple roles.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="concept-comparison-item" role="listitem">
        <span class="chip">GOOGLE ADK</span>
        <p>Cloud-native pick when Vertex AI Agent Builder is the deployment target.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="concept-comparison-item" role="listitem">
        <span class="chip">SEMANTIC KERNEL</span>
        <p>Cloud-native pick for Bedrock Agents / Microsoft Foundry Agent Service.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>
      None replace the safety rules below — a framework schedules the loop, it doesn't decide what the loop can
      touch. <a href="https://github.com/jthiruveedula/genai-data-platform/tree/main/labs/85-agents-langgraph-loop" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">A real, runnable lab</a> builds
      the LangGraph pattern end to end — a genuine <code>StateGraph</code> with a hard iteration budget and a
      human-approval gate, proven by a test suite that actually runs the loop.
    </p>

    <h2>Authenticating agents and MCP servers</h2>
    <div class="concept-flow" role="list" aria-label="Agent credential flow">
      <span class="chip chip--accent" role="listitem">AGENT</span>
      <span class="concept-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      <span class="chip" role="listitem">SCOPED BEARER TOKEN</span>
      <span class="concept-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      <span class="chip" role="listitem">MCP SERVER VALIDATES</span>
      <span class="concept-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      <span class="chip" role="listitem">TOOL EXECUTES</span>
    </div>
    <p>
      MCP requires any remote server to act as an <strong>OAuth 2.1 resource server</strong> — a separate
      authorization server issues the token, the MCP server only validates it and checks scopes. Every call carries a
      short-lived <code>Authorization: Bearer</code> token, never a long-lived API key — a leaked token expires and
      can't be replayed beyond its narrow scope.
    </p>
    <p>
      Least privilege applied to the credential itself: read-only scope on the one server a task needs, never an
      org-wide key. Per cloud: Workload Identity Federation (GCP), an IAM role via STS (AWS), a managed identity
      (Azure), or LiteLLM + an external OAuth provider (OSS). <a href="https://github.com/jthiruveedula/genai-data-platform/tree/main/labs/85-agents-mcp-oauth" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">A real, runnable lab</a> implements
      this exact gate — a FastAPI MCP server that mints/validates a scoped JWT and rejects an expired or
      under-scoped token, proven with a test suite that mints real signed tokens. <code>pip install -r requirements.txt && pytest -v</code>, no external identity provider required.
    </p>

    <h2>When RAG beats agents (and vice versa)</h2>
    <p>
      "Look this one thing up" → Module 25's single-hop RAG, faster and simpler. "Find X, compare it against last
      quarter, draft a summary" → an agent, planning across tool calls a single retrieval pass can't do. Which one a
      request needs is a design decision, not a default.
    </p>

    <h2>Safety: least privilege and human gates</h2>
    <p>
      An agent holds only the tools and scopes its task needs — a research agent doesn't need database write access.
      Any tool call with a real side effect (sending, deleting, spending) gets a human-approval gate first. Combined
      with Module 65's security lessons, this keeps a planning mistake or injected instruction from becoming a real
      consequence.
    </p>

    <h2>Try it in your flavor</h2>
    <FlavorTabs moduleId={moduleId} entries={entries} />

    <h2>Pitfalls</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Running an agent loop with no iteration budget — an unsolvable task will keep planning and acting indefinitely.</li>
      <li>Reaching for an agent on a single-hop lookup that Module 25's plain RAG API already answers faster and cheaper.</li>
      <li>Granting an agent broad tool access "for flexibility," which turns any planning mistake or injected instruction into a much larger blast radius.</li>
      <li>Letting an agent execute side-effecting tool calls (sending, deleting, spending) with no human approval step in between.</li>
      <li>Picking a framework by hype instead of fit — CrewAI's speed on a workflow that needed LangGraph's audit trail, or vice versa.</li>
      <li>Baking a long-lived API key into an agent's config instead of a scoped, short-lived OAuth token an MCP server can validate per call.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
  <ModuleQuiz moduleId={moduleId} />
  <ModuleRecap moduleId={moduleId} />
  <ModuleFooterNav moduleId={moduleId} />
</SiteLayout>
